  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM CDT Fri May 29 2026\n\nAbove normal temperatures and small chances for rain through Tuesday\n\nOmega block reminiscent pattern is in place currently but, luckily, \nshould slowly break down over the coming days and thus, cannot rule \nout occasional small chances for showers and thunderstorms, \nparticularly west of the Mississippi, as the upper ridge slides east \nand reduces in magnitude. The best shot during the next 3 days looks \nto be in those areas late Sunday night (20-50%).\n\nHowever, while a low possibility at this time (10% chance for rain, \nsupported mainly only by the 29.12z GFS), axis of 1000-1500 J/kg \nMLCAPE may sneak into our west Monday afternoon and evening with \nenough deep shear for organized convection and, given straight \nhodographs, a messy storm mode with risk for hail and damaging winds.\n\nHeights aloft rise again Tuesday as flow aloft transitions to a \nquasi-zonal pattern. Temperatures take a step upward into the mid \n80s.\n\nHigher rain chances return Wednesday into Friday\n\nShift to a more zonal pattern combined with a surface high located \nsomewhere in the SE CONUS should allow for both sufficient moisture \nto be advected northward and for some shortwaves to roll eastward \nover the region. This will allow for multiple chances for \nconvection and, depending on shear characteristics, a strong\nstorm.",
